Lots of viewers might have expected the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:08.080
person on this list to be on the very top. When&nbsp;
you think of actors who famously swear a lot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:12.480
the great Samuel L. Jackson is probably the&nbsp;
first person who comes to mind. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:16.400
according to Buzz Bingo, Jackson doesn't&nbsp;
actually hold the record for the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:21.680
foul-mouthed actor to ever grace the silver&nbsp;
screen. He may have delivered 301 swear words&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:26.080
over the course of his film career, but that&nbsp;
only gets him to number three on the list.

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:30.800
There are probably a few films that come to mind&nbsp;
when you imagine Jackson's most explicit roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:35.280
and there are certainly a number of different&nbsp;
films that contribute to his final swear count.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:39.600
The most profane character Jackson has ever&nbsp;
played in his career, is, unsurprisingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:46.000
a character in a Quentin Tarantino film. Ordell,&nbsp;
Jackson's character in Jackie Brown, swore 183&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:51.520
times over the course of the film, making him&nbsp;
the fifth most profane character in film history.

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:54.800
When Jimmy Fallon confronted Jackson&nbsp;
with the fact that he actually isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:58.400
the most profane actor in history,&nbsp;
Jackson had the perfect response:

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:04.160
"That's some bull—-. I mean, that's a —&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:05.601
I mean, I mean — no."

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:08.240
Indeed, Jackson has become so synonymous with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:12.080
swearing that InsiderHook reports the&nbsp;
most famous line in Snakes On A Plane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:17.069
was actually added after the fact in order&nbsp;
to placate fans who wanted to hear it.

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:24.400
"Enough is enough. I have had it with these&nbsp;
motherf—-ing snakes on this motherf—ing plane.”

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:27.352
That’s the line, right? Is&nbsp;
Monday a curse word yet?
 
 
 &nbsp;

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:31.520
Leonardo DiCaprio may be an actor known&nbsp;
for his impressive crying ability,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:34.080
but his swearing skills aren't too shabby either.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:39.520
As the runner-up on the list, DiCaprio's&nbsp;
swear count is a whopping 361 words.

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:44.560
Many of the swear words DiCaprio has uttered come&nbsp;
from Martin Scorsese's The Wolf of Wall Street.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:49.840
DiCaprio plays Jordan Belfort, a Wall Street&nbsp;
banker who makes a fortune at a young age,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:56.240
only to be hounded by the FBI for his wild antics&nbsp;
and debauchery. Jordan swears 332 times in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:01.280
film, making up a large chunk of DiCaprio's&nbsp;
total swear count. By Buzz Bingo's metric,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:06.640
Jordan is the most profane character in cinema&nbsp;
history. Unsurprisingly, The Wolf of Wall Street&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:12.880
is also the most vulgar film ever according&nbsp;
to the study, with 715 swear words overall.

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:13.710
"F— you!"

00:09:13.710 --> 00:09:18.320
"No, don't f—--- dare throw that f—---&nbsp;
water at me. Don't you f—--- dare."

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:22.640
Because not every swear word DiCaprio has said&nbsp;
comes from The Wolf of Wall Street, there are a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:27.760
few other films that must contribute to his swear&nbsp;
count. In the Tarantino film Once Upon a Time...In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:33.200
Hollywood, DiCaprio plays Rick Dalton, a washed-up&nbsp;
Western star struggling to stay relevant.

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:35.840
While the film may contain a&nbsp;
notable amount of swearing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:40.800
DiCaprio was reportedly insistent that the&nbsp;
swearing only happen on camera. According&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:45.280
to Fatherly, DiCaprio demanded that&nbsp;
the cast and crew stop swearing on set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:49.840
to protect his 10 year-old co-star, Julia&nbsp;
Butters. DiCaprio may have played the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:54.560
foul-mouthed character in film history, but he&nbsp;
draws the line at swearing around children.
 
 
 &nbsp;

